### How does Eureka work?

#### Registration and heartbeats

```java
// Spring Cloud Eureka client: auto-registers on startup
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableDiscoveryClient
public class TransferServiceApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(TransferServiceApplication.class, args);
    }
}
// application.yml:
// eureka.client.service-url.defaultZone: http://eureka-server:8761/eureka
// Instance registers, sends heartbeat every 30s, lease expires if missed
```

#### Client-side discovery

```java
// Caller fetches instances, load-balances locally
List<ServiceInstance> instances = discoveryClient.getInstances("transfer-service");
ServiceInstance chosen = instances.get(rng.nextInt(instances.size()));
String url = chosen.getUri() + "/api/v1/transfers";
```

### How does Kubernetes-native discovery work?

```yaml
# The Service IS the discovery mechanism
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: transfer-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: transfer
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8080
# Caller just does: http://transfer-service/api/v1/transfers
# CoreDNS resolves it to ready pod endpoints
```

### How do they compare?

| Aspect | Eureka | Kubernetes |
|---|---|---|
| Registration | Application heartbeats (library) | Platform readiness probes |
| Discovery | Client queries registry | DNS resolution (CoreDNS) |
| Load balancing | Client-side | Server-side (kube-proxy) |
| Extra server | Eureka cluster required | None (platform-native) |
| Consistency model | AP (keeps stale registrations) | Endpoint controller (consistent) |
| Works where | Anywhere with a network | Inside K8s cluster only |

### When do you still need Eureka?

```java
// Off-Kubernetes: services on VMs or bare metal → need Eureka
// Multi-cloud: no single K8s cluster spans all environments → need Eureka
// Gradual migration: some services in K8s, some on VMs → Eureka as the bridge
// Fully on single K8s cluster: Eureka is redundant complexity
```

### What is self-preservation?

During a network partition, Eureka stops evicting instances because it cannot tell 'instance died' from 'network blip.' It keeps stale registrations on the theory that a stale list is safer than an empty one.

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: adding Eureka in a greenfield single-cluster K8s deployment
// K8s already provides discovery; Eureka adds a server to operate for no benefit

// WRONG: hardcoding instance addresses
String url = "http://10.0.1.42:8080/api/transfers"; // dies when instance moves

// CORRECT: use logical service names resolved by discovery
String url = "http://transfer-service/api/transfers";
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Service discovery is the same problem everywhere: find healthy instances and route to them.
- The right tool is whatever already owns your runtime: K8s-native when on K8s, Eureka when not.
- During migration, Eureka bridges both worlds; retire it per service once fully in-cluster.
- Never hardcode addresses; always resolve by logical name.
